#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205066} :activity/id #uuid "c9d7f129-88d5-5598-a986-8d3bfc071ee1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048851 :topic/id #uuid "c6169265-da51-5b16-b02d-ab34a29c4b9d" :topic/math-academy-id 5536} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205178 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll extend our knowledge of gradients of scalar-valued functions.\n\nRecall that, for a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ that maps a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ to a scalar value $f(\\mathbf{x})$, the *gradient* of $f$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ is defined as the column vector of partial derivatives:\n\n![](images/4b/4ba97eb3ffac5de1b569716f0ccaa648749248b62e4918c641f93de84b52b61d.svg)\n\n**Note:** Some resources use $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\mathbf x}$ to denote the gradient. We shall avoid this notation.\n\nRecall that the *total derivative* of a scalar function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with respect to a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is the row vector\n\n![](images/9b/9b02d1184f009b66095b1bfd9be6d4d0f29aeb41a0ba08469b404ffc8ca6e88d.svg)\n\nHence, the gradient is simply the transpose of the total derivative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}) = \\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n$$\n\nWe'll now discuss some important results related to gradients widely used in machine learning applications.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4279a9cc-57eb-59f6-aa64-7171ceecd132" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11724 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d0e7f1e0-91ce-52b4-b0eb-01ddfafd3727" :step/math-academy-id 610949 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205179 :tutorial/content "Let’s derive a general rule for the gradient of a linear function.\n\nSuppose $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n,$ and the scalar function $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{b}^T \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a fixed vector. \n\nWe wish to find an expression for the gradient of $f.$\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f = \\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{b}^T \\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's write our vectors $\\mathbf b$ and $\\mathbf x$ explicitly:\n\n![](images/8a/8a824abda10928a55f4efd94f7c0e4bff0dabf13fc44b789ed53e829971c3471.svg)\n\nThen, $\\mathbf b^T \\mathbf x$ is the scalar function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{T}x & =[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & … & {b}_{n}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf b^T \\mathbf x\n=b_1x_1+ b_2x_2 + \\cdots + b_nx_n.\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative of $\\mathbf b^T \\mathbf x$ with respect to $x_i$ is $b_i,$ since all the other terms will vanish. \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x_i}\\left(\\mathbf b^T\\mathbf x\\right) = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x_i}\\left(b_1x_1+ b_2x_2 + \\cdots + b_i x_i + \\cdots + b_nx_n\\right) = b_i\n$$\n\nSince this is true for all $1\\leq i\\leq n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf x} \\left(\\mathbf b^T \\mathbf x\\right) = \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nThis is the multivariable analog of the familiar rule from single-variable calculus:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dx}(a x) = a\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c25aa9a7-caaa-5673-8abb-2fbe809b2b6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11725 :tutorial/title "Gradient of a Linear Form With Respect to a Vector"} :step/id #uuid "2c532b4d-28a1-5e4f-bf4e-c704d39bc43b" :step/math-academy-id 610950 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205068}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204102 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203856 :question/id #uuid "8ad540bc-bc15-5b57-aa7a-1ea46693a4e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-17348"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6600adba-92a6-523c-b214-9e506bc37cc9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050832}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532701}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Gradients of Linear Forms"} :step/id #uuid "413a861c-615a-5d66-9848-7a90e318f1aa" :step/math-academy-id 610951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205200 :tutorial/content "We have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf w} \\left(\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w\\right) = 2\\mathbf w\n$$\n\nThis result is analogous to the following result from single-variable calculus:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d w}(w^2) = 2w\n$$\n\nTo see why this is true, let $\\mathbf w \\in\\mathbb R^n$ be given by\n![](images/ab/abacc87863d62c768803174901464f57748f1488c3518a25cefc06a411ab31f9.svg)\n\nThen, $\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w$ is the *scalar* function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{T}w & =[\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} & {w}_{2} & … & {w}_{n}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {w}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w\n=w_1^2+ w_2^2 + \\cdots + w_n^2.\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative of $\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w$ with respect to $w_i$ is $2w_i,$ since all the other terms will vanish. Since this is true for all $1\\leq i\\leq n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf w} \\left(\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w\\right) = 2\\mathbf w\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nFinally, note that since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w = ||\\mathbf w ||^2\n$$\n\nwe have the following equivalent result:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf w}(||\\mathbf w ||^2) = 2\\mathbf w\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3b673551-e6e2-5f5a-9396-46f3667d7c89" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13110 :tutorial/title "The Gradient of a Squared Norm"} :step/id #uuid "06c67dd9-2eca-5c7b-9f34-3068dc6429c3" :step/math-academy-id 610954 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204103 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203864 :question/id #uuid "d50d20a4-2c3e-5469-8ced-2fde5d6690e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-17754"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7de7a9a-4848-5590-b066-f746262181e5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050758}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544884}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Gradients Including Squared Norms"} :step/id #uuid "f14993e7-fcd4-5c28-9b86-6a16f4aa4af6" :step/math-academy-id 610955 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205071}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205194 :tutorial/content "Consider the $\\textrm{L}_1$ (Manhattan) norm of a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, defined as\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf{x}\\|_1 = \\sum_{i=1}^n |x_i|.\n$$\n\nWe want to compute the gradient of this function with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$. To do that, let’s focus on finding the partial derivative with respect to a single component $x_k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂‖x{‖}_{1}}{∂{x}_{k}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{k}}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}|{x}_{i}| \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{k}}(|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+⋯+|{x}_{k}|+⋯+|{x}_{n}|) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂|{x}_{1}|}{∂{x}_{k}}+\\frac{∂|{x}_{2}|}{∂{x}_{k}}+⋯+\\frac{∂|{x}_{k}|}{∂{x}_{k}}+⋯+\\frac{∂|{x}_{n}|}{∂{x}_{k}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂|{x}_{k}|}{∂{x}_{k}} \\\\  & =\\text{sign}({x}_{k}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{sign}(x_k)$ is defined as\n\n![](images/88/88b4650c41c9ab38ea82851481ed720821ad7f93bfeac56a93d70effe680f230.svg)\n\nSo, the full gradient of the $\\textrm{L}_1$ norm is simply\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} \\|\\mathbf{x}\\|_1 = \\text{sign}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nwhich is a vector whose $k$th component is $\\text{sign}(x_k)$. \n\nThe gradient of $\\|\\mathbf x\\|_1$ is not defined at points where any component of $\\mathbf x$ equals zero. This is because the absolute value function has a sharp corner at zero and is therefore not differentiable there. Various methods are available to overcome this difficulty when the derivative of the $\\textrm L1$ norm is used in practice, and we'll discuss these in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "c7eca8be-6193-55ed-88bd-4a8d9da9ace1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12455 :tutorial/title "The Gradient of the L1 Norm"} :step/id #uuid "e191dbfe-d4c9-5d33-8860-ed59f2198e57" :step/math-academy-id 610958 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205073}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204104 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203874 :question/id #uuid "5222f4d3-4fec-545c-b9f3-4d8a8e84d2c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-18710"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86b3efb7-511d-5da7-92ca-5add976b1ca1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544835}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Gradients of Functions Containing L1 Norms"} :step/id #uuid "43a1d542-6849-59f1-92a3-0e22fc728d48" :step/math-academy-id 610959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205219 :tutorial/content "In many machine learning models, a scalar function depends on a vector that itself depends on another vector.\n\nSuppose $f:\\mathbb{R}^m \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\mathbf g:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m,$ and define\n\n$$\nh(\\mathbf x)=f(\\mathbf g(\\mathbf x)).\n$$\n\nThen the **chain rule for gradients** states:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf x} h(\\mathbf x)\n=\nJ_{\\mathbf g}(\\mathbf x)^T\n\\,\\nabla_{\\mathbf u} f(\\mathbf u)\\Big|_{\\mathbf u=\\mathbf g(\\mathbf x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $J_{\\mathbf g}(\\mathbf x)$ is the Jacobian of $\\mathbf g.$\n\nIn other words, we have  \n\n$$\n\\bigg(\\substack{\\Large \\text{Gradient of} \\[3pt] \\Large\\text{composition}}\\bigg)   \n= \\bigg(\\substack{\\Large \\text{Jacobian of} \\[3pt] \\Large\\text{the inner function}}\\bigg)^T \n\\cdot \\:\\:\n\\bigg(\\substack{\\Large \\text{Gradient of} \\[3pt] \\Large\\text{the outer function}}\\bigg).\n$$\n\nLet’s see this in action." :tutorial/id #uuid "bb55879e-d159-5a54-914b-d601dddb23dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14946 :tutorial/title "Chain Rule to Vector Gradients"} :step/id #uuid "85adcd58-6472-52c1-8d71-dd29e52d2aba" :step/math-academy-id 610962 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205072}}] :activity/title "Vector Gradients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204175}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544922} :activity/id #uuid "fe03f318-7113-59e6-9fe3-2a4d3bcad045" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048851 :topic/id #uuid "c6169265-da51-5b16-b02d-ab34a29c4b9d" :topic/math-academy-id 5536} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d839084-ebf5-50a1-84e8-aaab3647d2f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544187} {:answer/id #uuid "3cdd1bc7-4812-57f7-81c8-e65554be4222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544223} {:answer/id #uuid "6a10b282-678e-5e67-8c59-8dfb7a853cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3‖w‖" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544316} {:answer/id #uuid "8cdc6452-7362-5379-a198-0f6e095833c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544389} {:answer/id #uuid "c4904ffb-8abe-5c81-add0-ac73a2f9a0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6‖w‖" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544504}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544187} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2d8ec61-54a3-524d-ac1c-f8499eaaacb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "17d82828-3c8a-5034-bf42-fab4e2f3cbb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-265744" :question/problem "Given the function \n\n$$\nf(w)=-3‖w{‖}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nthat maps a vector to a scalar, find ${∇}_{w}f.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the result \n\n$$\n{∇}_{w}({w}^{T}w)=2w,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}f & ={∇}_{w}(-3‖w{‖}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}^{2})-{∇}_{w}(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {∇}_{w}({w}^{T}w)-{∇}_{w}(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2w+0 \\\\  & =-6w.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "45be477a-4758-5690-b800-0acb22740f26" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544947}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544947 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a1d37be-3fa0-59b9-bd20-4310f8e5627e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-2w+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544110} {:answer/id #uuid "303bfee8-5463-56bb-9681-e3b9d4158949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-2w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544193} {:answer/id #uuid "588514db-1798-58bb-9d0c-b764080a8b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544282} {:answer/id #uuid "d3532fcf-148d-5c02-9530-dfe46187e43d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-2‖w‖" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544535} {:answer/id #uuid "dd3e8051-4283-52b3-93e7-0409ffbc90da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544564}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544193} :answer-field/id #uuid "669218e9-dbb9-5cbc-b3fc-b7445d8b9e17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d8c2cd33-feb8-5850-af19-6e3631c29b25" :question/math-academy-id "q-265746" :question/problem "Given the function \n\n$$\nf(x,w)=3{w}^{T}x-‖w{‖}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nthat maps two vectors to a scalar, find ${∇}_{w}f.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the results \n\n$$\n{∇}_{w}({w}^{T}x)=x,\\,{∇}_{w}({w}^{T}w)=2w,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}f & ={∇}_{w}(3{w}^{T}x-‖w{‖}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {∇}_{w}({w}^{T}x)-{∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}^{2})+{∇}_{w}(1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {∇}_{w}({w}^{T}x)-{∇}_{w}({w}^{T}w)+{∇}_{w}(1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot x-2w+0 \\\\  & =3x-2w.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7fbe5f30-b984-5b5a-bcf7-21b4ac60ddeb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544992}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544969 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544835 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fb156c9-d84f-5baa-add4-3f456c7cf40a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(‖w{‖}_{1})^{3}\\cdot \\text{sign}(w)-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544434} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ef1186-89a0-5fdf-84b4-0d9057dc9b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(‖w{‖}_{1})^{3}\\cdot \\text{sign}(w)-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544472} {:answer/id #uuid "bd827637-7b44-5716-8016-3b379e1ce6d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(‖w{‖}_{1})^{2}\\cdot \\text{sign}(w)-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544490} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5a0474-5015-51cb-829b-d6477c15697d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(‖w{‖}_{1})^{2}\\cdot \\text{sign}(w)-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544514} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e63be3-9e49-5062-b7d5-c7b2c1a4ae27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(‖w{‖}_{1})^{2}\\cdot \\text{sign}(w)-4w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544587}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544490} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5042304-8241-5082-82cb-26bee7d6fc07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7dc41e95-539c-50b9-8fb0-b63764e8684c" :question/math-academy-id "q-275978" :question/problem "Given the function \n\n$$\nf(x,w)={(‖w{‖}_{1})}^{3}-4{x}^{T}w\n$$\n\nthat maps a vector to a scalar, find ${∇}_{w}f.$ You may assume that $w$ has no zero components." :question/worked-solution "Using the results\n\n$$\n{∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}_{1})=\\text{sign}(w),\\,{∇}_{w}({x}^{T}w)=x,\n$$\n\n we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}f & ={∇}_{w}({(‖w{‖}_{1})}^{3}-4{x}^{T}w) \\\\  & ={∇}_{w}({(‖w{‖}_{1})}^{3})-{∇}_{w}(4{x}^{T}w) \\\\  & ={∇}_{w}({(‖w{‖}_{1})}^{3})-4\\cdot {∇}_{w}({x}^{T}w) \\\\  & =3(‖w{‖}_{1})^{2}\\cdot {∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}_{1})-4\\cdot x \\\\  & =3(‖w{‖}_{1})^{2}\\cdot \\text{sign}(w)-4\\cdot x \\\\  & =3(‖w{‖}_{1})^{2}\\cdot \\text{sign}(w)-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c0ed2fb4-d964-5c56-afa4-a060d0c3984c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25c2a063-df9f-56e6-b378-7e460f0097d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{sign}(w)-2‖w‖" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544172} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd2e5ee-b76f-520f-84c9-af1e8bcd6860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{sign}(w)-2w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544184} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee0c7f8-2f47-540f-b5d4-0d2509ab1dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{sign}(w)-w" :db/id #edb/ref 1